// Sapphire's default day (M1 W1), compiled into `sapphire_world`: `TodTimeline::default_day`,
// whose module docs say how keys blend. Packs (A4) will carry timelines as content.
//
// - Eight keys: midnight, the blue hour, sunrise, day, afternoon, golden hour, sunset and dusk.
//   Each sets every group, so this can be the base timeline; between keys every group blends
//   linearly in time (colors in OkLab, directions along great circles).
// - 10:00 is exactly the renderer's default look: the sun the sandbox has always drawn with,
//   `SkyGradient::default()` and `PostParams::default()`. A scene that doesn't choose a time
//   starts there, so its frames are what they were before time of day; all but the fog (M1 W3),
//   which the renderer's default leaves out and 10:00 keeps as a light haze, so scenes from
//   before fog pin it off (the sandbox's `--fog off`).
// - Fog (M1 W3): the far layer's color is a tint on the sky's horizon, white or nearly, so the
//   distance melts into the sky's own color at every hour; near haze and height fog carry the
//   hour's mist (peach at sunrise, lavender in the twilights, deep blue at night), thickest in the
//   morning, light by day. The moon lights the night's fog a little (`sun_inscatter` follows the
//   key light). Characters take 40 to 50% of it (`characters`), so they read as figures where
//   the world melts away.
// - Directions are the way light travels, from the body into the scene. Looking from the
//   sandbox's camera, the sun rises on the left, passes high on the camera's side (lighting the
//   character from the front), and sets on the right; the moon rises on the left as the sun
//   sets, and sets on the right as it rises, at most 38° up (at midnight). The key light follows
//   the sun by day and the moon at night, turning across the camera's side in the two twilights.
// - A body lights the scene only from above the horizon, so each one's intensity reaches 0 at
//   the key where it reaches the horizon (the sun at 5:00 and 20:00, the moon at 18:45), and
//   stays 0 below it.
// - The moon's light is a clear blue, and at midnight about one and a half times its luminance at
//   dusk and the blue hour. Painted ground is yellow-green, with little blue to reflect, so under
//   it a lawn goes a dark, cool teal while stone and plaster turn blue; and riding no higher than
//   38°, the moon lights the walls and characters facing the camera more than the ground. Retune
//   it with the ground in view: a moon barely bluer than white, twice as bright at midnight as at
//   dusk and 58° up, lights a lawn five times as strongly as at dusk, green and on screen about
//   half as bright as by day.
// - Characters get a key light of their own (`character`; C2 draws it) that follows the world's
//   key light through the day, as WuWa fits character brightness to its environment: the same
//   light by day, and a little brighter from golden hour to sunrise, so characters read. In
//   luminance it is about 1.5 times the key light at sunset, dusk and the blue hour, where the
//   world is darkest and its low moon only grazes it, and 1.25 times under the midnight moon,
//   where the night is brightest. While the sun or moon crosses the horizon, time of day fades the
//   key with the world's light (`TodLook::character_key`), so the ratio holds through the
//   crossings. Exposure lifts characters and world alike, so that ratio is what the screen shows:
//   at night characters take a cool key at a third (dusk, the blue hour) to a half (midnight) of
//   their noon brightness, a step above the world. Retune it with the sun, the moon and exposure:
//   a night key set apart from the world's light keeps characters at their noon brightness once
//   exposure lifts the night, while the world drops to under half.
// - The sky's layers (M1 W2): `discs` are the sun's and moon's discs and glows, apart from the
//   light the bodies give, so a setting sun's disc fades on the horizon after its light, and the
//   moon shows pale as it rises at sunset. Glows are wide and warm at sunrise and sunset and
//   spread along the horizon there; the dawn and dusk keys keep a glow where the sun is below
//   the horizon. `stars` come out through dusk, fullest at midnight. `clouds` are painted in two
//   tones with a rim: white tops over lavender-blue shade by day, peach and gold over violet at
//   sunrise and sunset, dark blue with moonlit rims at night; the cirrus is fullest at sunrise and
//   sunset, where it catches the color.
// - Post's compositing layers (M1 P2), at every key; for everything R3 and W1 drew, 10:00 is
//   still exactly the renderer's default look. The `diffusion` glow is light and clean by day,
//   dreamiest and warmest at sunrise and sunset (peach, gold, orange), lavender through the
//   twilights and a cool moonlit blue at night. Characters bloom above a higher threshold than
//   the world (×1.3 to ×1.5) at half its strength or less, so faces hold while the world glows.
//   The three `gradients` keep their shapes and modes all day, so they blend smoothly: the top of
//   the frame multiplied toward the sky's blue (violet at sunset, indigo at night), the bottom
//   multiplied toward the ground's shade (lavender by day, a deep violet at sunset), framing the
//   character, and a soft-light pool of the hour's light around the middle. The `lens_flare` is
//   warm at sunrise and sunset and off at night; the vignette is tinted navy, violet or a warm
//   dark at night and sunset.
// - Every number is a starting point for art direction, not a measurement.
